WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.443
The Knowledge Lifecycle of Large Language
Models.

2
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:10.019
Give a language model a document that
contradicts what it learned.

3
00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:11.486
Which one wins?

4
00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:16.302
Retrieval works only when the document
beats what the model already believes.

5
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:22.160
A fact passes through five stages:
acquire, store, retrieve, update and

6
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:22.641
forget.

7
00:00:23.041 --> 00:00:25.622
The failure sits between retrieval and
update.

8
00:00:26.022 --> 00:00:29.969
The document arrives, and nothing tells
the model which copy to trust.

9
00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:35.565
Take a fact every model holds, then hand
it a document that says otherwise.

10
00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.103
Its memory says Paris.

11
00:00:38.103 --> 00:00:39.706
The document says Lyon.

12
00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:44.351
864 forward passes, across 6 open models.

13
00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.302
Same question, same document.

14
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.723
Qwen2.5-1.5B adopts the document and
answers Lyon.

15
00:00:53.123 --> 00:00:54.744
TinyLlama keeps Paris.

16
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:01.123
Across 304 genuine conflicts, the document
wins two times in three.

17
00:01:01.523 --> 00:01:03.458
66.8% adopt it.

18
00:01:03.458 --> 00:01:07.069
27.3% keep their own answer.

19
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.524
Holding a fact scales with size.

20
00:01:10.524 --> 00:01:12.355
Using the document does not.

21
00:01:12.755 --> 00:01:17.875
TinyLlama holds almost every fact, yet
adopts the document least of all six.

22
00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:23.011
Qwen2.5-0.5B, less than half its size,
adopts it most: 91.8%.

23
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.828
Now change only the wording.

24
00:01:26.228 --> 00:01:32.031
TinyLlama's adoption moves from 17.4% to
75.0%.

25
00:01:32.431 --> 00:01:36.548
A single-phrasing result reports the
sentence as much as the model.

26
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.234
Probability is not an answer.

27
00:01:40.775 --> 00:01:43.809
Lyon carries 0.19 of the probability.

28
00:01:43.809 --> 00:01:46.023
The model still says Paris.

29
00:01:46.423 --> 00:01:52.382
Above a tenth of the probability, the
document's answer goes unsaid 20.8% of the

30
00:01:52.382 --> 00:01:52.759
time.

31
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:55.799
That gap is not hypothetical.

32
00:01:56.199 --> 00:02:00.353
An earlier version of this work read
probability alone, and reached a

33
00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.919
conclusion the measurement did not
support.

34
00:02:03.319 --> 00:02:05.452
This paper corrects it, on the record.

35
00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:09.777
By Amey Thakur and Sarvesh Talele.

36
00:02:10.177 --> 00:02:12.694
All code, probes and measurements are
public.
